MODIFIED ESP8089 FIRMWARE — experimental TXR8 compact-completion candidate, 2026-10-19

This is a modified derivative of Espressif System's ESP8089
firmware/eagle_fw_first_init_v19.bin (SHA2561b8b7f93f40d3c2dd7ab7f3e2d29cd118604249a5926a320163bf358254e4162).
The original firmware and newly written extensions are Apache-2.0; see
LICENSE-firmware.txt. Original stage1 calibration firmware is unchanged.
Vendor permission / attribution:
https://github.com/al177/esp8089/commit/0fd883fe43d969d1fcf159d2e4a4a8e7c04200b0

Changes: retained radio-completion reports with host cursor acknowledgement,
replay and explicit capability negotiation; live calibrated power ceiling;
read-only diagnostic access; saved interrupt-state protection; a capability-
installed RAM code overlay reusing retired startup/INIT/debug functions;
bounded per-packet DATA-attempt counts and last observed transmit PHY,
including aggregate members and excluding CTS/BAR exchanges. Unknown final
attempt histories are explicitly marked. This does not provide every earlier
per-rate attempt or an absolute radiated-power measurement.

Source, fixed-original builder, linker layout, copy plans and output hashes
identify these modifications. This is not a complete source release of the
original firmware. No ESP8266 SDK object, chip ROM dump, credentials or Xiaomi
installed firmware image is included. All hardware tests use temporary RAM.

The Linux consumer has its own GPL-2.0 license; it is not part of this firmware
binary. Experimental status and validation limits are recorded separately.

Additional modification: bounded, IRQ-safe allocation for new completion and
power-reply events preserves an original control buffer in the shared ROM pool.
It reuses retired PP initialization space only after capability installation.

Retained-completion capacity continues in every fixed-size reply; this
candidate retains 32 records and uses the matching TXR7 Linux consumer.
Ring size is unchanged from TXR6 W32 (256 extra bytes relative to TXR5).
Event payloads remain 52 bytes, with two records per event and the original
shared four-buffer pool unchanged.

TXR7 additionally batches completion notifications at four records and uses
a distinct explicit partial-flush flag in Linux polls. Original radio data,
credit geometry, event size and control-buffer reserve are unchanged.

TXR8 compact-completion candidate, 2026-10-19: each retained completion is
8bytes (sequence and packed outcome/NoACK/known-attempt-count/final-PHY),
replacing16byte raw metadata records. Four records now fit a single unchanged
52byte event. Distinct TXR8 magic prevents pairing with old consumers.
The4record batching threshold,32record retention,52byte payload,64byte shared
ROM buffers and original control reserve remain unchanged. Full raw descriptor
flags and the redundant unobserved PHY byte are no longer exported. Unknown
histories stay explicitly unknown; invalid outcomes fail closed.
Includes terminal aggregate-stop counters; original ROM reclaim is unwrapped.
Performance-only RAM candidate; no stability acceptance or promotion.
